explain.narrative:
## Core concept
CROSS JOIN combines every row from the first table with every row from the second table. If the inputs contain m and n rows, the result contains m × n rows. It does not require a matching-key condition.

## SQLite syntax
```sql
SELECT columns
FROM table_a
CROSS JOIN table_b;
```

## Worked examples
### 1. Create the two independent input tables
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS regions;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, base_price INTEGER NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE regions (region_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, region_name TEXT NOT NULL, shipping_fee INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',50),(2,'Desk Lamp',200);
INSERT INTO regions VALUES (1,'North',20),(2,'South',25),(3,'East',15);
SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products) AS product_count, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM regions) AS region_count;
```
Expected result: 2 | 3

The sample contains two products and three shipping regions.

### 2. Generate every product and region combination
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS regions;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, base_price INTEGER NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE regions (region_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, region_name TEXT NOT NULL, shipping_fee INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',50),(2,'Desk Lamp',200);
INSERT INTO regions VALUES (1,'North',20),(2,'South',25),(3,'East',15);
SELECT p.product_name, r.region_name FROM products AS p CROSS JOIN regions AS r ORDER BY p.product_id, r.region_id;
```
Expected result: Notebook | North; Notebook | South; Notebook | East; Desk Lamp | North; Desk Lamp | South; Desk Lamp | East

A CROSS JOIN returns the Cartesian product: two product rows multiplied by three region rows gives six combinations.

### 3. Calculate a regional price for each combination
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS regions;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, base_price INTEGER NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE regions (region_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, region_name TEXT NOT NULL, shipping_fee INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',50),(2,'Desk Lamp',200);
INSERT INTO regions VALUES (1,'North',20),(2,'South',25),(3,'East',15);
SELECT p.product_name, r.region_name, p.base_price + r.shipping_fee AS delivered_price FROM products AS p CROSS JOIN regions AS r ORDER BY p.product_id, r.region_id;
```
Expected result: Notebook | North | 70; Notebook | South | 75; Notebook | East | 65; Desk Lamp | North | 220; Desk Lamp | South | 225; Desk Lamp | East | 215

The result can be used to create a product-by-region pricing matrix.

### 4. Filter combinations after the Cartesian product
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS regions;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, base_price INTEGER NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE regions (region_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, region_name TEXT NOT NULL, shipping_fee INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',50),(2,'Desk Lamp',200);
INSERT INTO regions VALUES (1,'North',20),(2,'South',25),(3,'East',15);
SELECT p.product_name, r.region_name FROM products AS p CROSS JOIN regions AS r WHERE r.shipping_fee <= 20 ORDER BY p.product_id, r.region_id;
```
Expected result: Notebook | North; Notebook | East; Desk Lamp | North; Desk Lamp | East

The WHERE condition reduces the generated combinations to regions whose fee is at most 20.

### 5. Count combinations for each product
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS regions;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS products;
CREATE TABLE products (product_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, product_name TEXT NOT NULL, base_price INTEGER NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE regions (region_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, region_name TEXT NOT NULL, shipping_fee INTEGER NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1,'Notebook',50),(2,'Desk Lamp',200);
INSERT INTO regions VALUES (1,'North',20),(2,'South',25),(3,'East',15);
SELECT p.product_name, COUNT(r.region_id) AS available_regions FROM products AS p CROSS JOIN regions AS r GROUP BY p.product_id, p.product_name ORDER BY p.product_id;
```
Expected result: Notebook | 3; Desk Lamp | 3

Each product is paired with every region, so each product has three combinations.

## Query behaviour and safety
A CROSS JOIN has no ON condition. Every row on the left is paired with every row on the right. The output size is the product of input row counts, before later filtering. If either table is unexpectedly large, the result can consume substantial time and memory. Use explicit CROSS JOIN syntax to make the Cartesian intent clear.

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting that the row count multiplies.
- Using CROSS JOIN when a key-based INNER JOIN was intended.
- Assuming output order without ORDER BY.
- Failing to filter a generated combination set when only a subset is required.
- Overlooking duplicate rows in either input.

## Best practices
Estimate m × n before running a query. Use small controlled inputs for learning. Add clear filters and select only needed columns. For large production workloads, review the query plan and resource impact before generating combinations.

